  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nHigh pressure centered over the eastern Great Lakes will continue to\ntranslate eastward tonight into tomorrow. As this occurs, low level \nflow will turn and become southerly on Tuesday. Meanwhile, mid level \nhigh pressure will expand eastward tomorrow but then start to get \nsuppressed southward on Wednesday.\n\nThis combination will result in a fairly quick warm up as well as an\ninflux of higher dew points making it more humid. It will be dry \ninto at least early to mid afternoon Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nA short wave will move from the upper Mississippi Valley eastwards\nacross the upper Great Lakes Wednesday into Wednesday night. This\nwill help force a cold front southeast, entering the forecast area\nWednesday night. There is some uncertainty whether any convection can\ndevelop ahead of the boundary Wednesday afternoon and affect areas\nnorth/west of I-71. If it does, then there could be a narrow window\nlate Wednesday afternoon into early Wednesday evening, when\ninstability is maximized, that could result in some strong to severe\nstorms. \n\nLarger concern is for locally heavy rainfall. Precipitable water\nahead of the boundary is forecast to increase to 1.8 to 2.0 inches.\nThe warm cloud layer will become deep (great than 4 km) and a modest\nlow level jet will be translating across the area Wednesday night. In\naddition, low level flow will veer and become nearly parallel to the\nboundary, as the latter only makes slow southward progress as mid\nlevel support moves away from the region. This could lead to some \nlocations getting repeated showers and storms overnight.\n\nThe front will still be draped across the area on Thursday,\ncontinuing to move slowly and not getting much push until a second \nshort wave crosses the Ohio Valley late Thursday into Thursday \nevening. It appears that there may be a relative minimum in activity \non Thursday morning, or at least a decrease in intensity of rainfall.\nBut then more robust convection redevelops Thursday afternoon with \ndiurnal heating and the increase in mid level forcing. This will \nbring a renewed threat of heavy rain and training cells, primarily \nacross the southern counties. \n\nAll of this activity will push southeast of the area, along with the\nfront, on Thursday evening. Cooler and drier conditions will return\nfor the end of the week.",
